{"subjectId":"javascript","id":"higher-order-functions","title":"Higher-Order Functions & Callbacks","group":"Functions & Scope","order":19,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A higher-order function takes a function as an argument or returns one. It is the core of functional-style JavaScript.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Higher-order function</strong> — a function that <strong>accepts</strong> a function (a <em>callback</em>) and/or <strong>returns</strong> a function. <code>map</code>, <code>filter</code>, <code>setTimeout</code>, <code>addEventListener</code> and <code>bind</code> are all higher-order.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-down\"></i>Taking a function</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function repeat(n, action) {\n  for (let i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) action(i);\n}\nrepeat(3, (i) =&gt; console.log(\"tick\", i));\n\nconst applyAll = (value, ...fns) =&gt; fns.map((f) =&gt; f(value));\nconsole.log(applyAll(9, Math.sqrt, (x) =&gt; x * 2, String));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>tick 0\ntick 1\ntick 2\n[ 3, 18, '9' ]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-up\"></i>Returning a function</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const greaterThan = (n) =&gt; (m) =&gt; m &gt; n;\nconst isAdult = greaterThan(17);\nconsole.log([12, 18, 30].filter(isAdult));\n\nfunction logged(fn) {\n  return function (...args) {\n    const result = fn.apply(this, args);\n    console.log(`${fn.name}(${args.join(\", \")}) = ${result}`);\n    return result;\n  };\n}\nconst add = logged(function add(a, b) { return a + b; });\nadd(2, 3);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[ 18, 30 ]\nadd(2, 3) = 5</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-clock\"></i>Synchronous vs asynchronous callbacks</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>console.log(\"start\");\n[1, 2].forEach((n) =&gt; console.log(\"sync callback\", n));   // runs now\nsetTimeout(() =&gt; console.log(\"async callback\"), 0);        // runs later\nconsole.log(\"end\");</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>start\nsync callback 1\nsync callback 2\nend\nasync callback</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>The <code>parseInt</code> trap</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>console.log([\"1\", \"2\", \"3\"].map(parseInt));\nconsole.log([\"1\", \"2\", \"3\"].map(Number));\nconsole.log([\"1\", \"2\", \"3\"].map((s) =&gt; parseInt(s, 10)));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[ 1, NaN, NaN ]\n[ 1, 2, 3 ]\n[ 1, 2, 3 ]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>map</code> calls its callback with <code>(element, index, array)</code>. <code>parseInt</code> treats the index as the radix: <code>parseInt('2', 1)</code> → <code>NaN</code>. Wrap callbacks whose extra parameters mean something else.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is a callback function?</summary><div><p>A function passed as an argument to another function, to be called by it — either synchronously (array methods) or later (timers, events, I/O).</p></div></details></section>"}